          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"“Jew” ordinarily denotes Jewish ethnic, ancestral, or religious identity, while in notable theological contexts it can identify an inwardly faithful member of God’s covenant people. The exact scope of that inward sense is disputed.\",\"book_summary\":\"“Jew” denotes one belonging to Judah at contextual scales ranging from the tribe and Israel collectively to the ethnic people and the inwardly faithful subset.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A member of the Jewish/covenant people, with context distinguishing ordinary ethnic identity from authentic inward covenant identity.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"One belonging to Judah, contextually identified at tribal, national, ethnic, or inwardly faithful levels.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary conflict appears. The book organizes tribal, collective, ethnic, and inward uses under one contextual definition, whereas the consensus presents literal and inward senses separately and emphasizes disagreement over whether the inward category includes faithful Jews, all Christians, or the Church. The book does not clearly resolve that disputed sub-boundary.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both recognize ordinary historical-ethnic usage and the inward-Jew distinction of Romans 2:28–29.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum cores, both readings identify a Jew as someone belonging to the Judah/Israel covenantal people, while allowing an inwardly faithful sense distinguished from merely outward identity. The book’s scale-based formulation synthesizes cases already recognized in the consensus rather than replacing, narrowing, or materially expanding its central referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The book’s umbrella phrase “belonging to Judah” may imply a tighter unity among tribal, ethnic, collective-Israel, and inward senses than the cited texts establish, and it leaves unclear whether Gentile Christians can count as inward Jews. That ambiguity, however, mirrors rather than contradicts the consensus’s acknowledged dispute.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
